---
import ContentLayout from '../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro';
import CardGrid from '../../components/CardGrid.astro';
import { routePath } from '../../i18n/routes';

const locale = 'en' as const;
const p = (key: Parameters<typeof routePath>[1]) => routePath(locale, key);

const services = [
  {
    icon: 'profile',
    href: p('services/gbp'),
    title: 'Google Business Profile Management',
    text: 'Categories, services, photos, posts and reviews. Most of what decides your Maps ranking sits inside the profile, not the website.',
  },
  {
    icon: 'map',
    href: p('services/local-seo'),
    title: 'Local SEO',
    text: 'The umbrella programme that points your profile, your site and your external trust signals at the same target.',
  },
  {
    icon: 'grid',
    href: p('services/rank-tracking'),
    title: 'Map Rank Tracking',
    text: 'Not one average number — a 7×7 grid laid over your city, showing where you actually rank block by block.',
  },
  {
    icon: 'wrench',
    href: p('services/technical-seo'),
    title: 'Technical SEO',
    text: 'Crawlability, indexing, speed and structured data. However good the content is, none of it ranks if these are broken.',
  },
  {
    icon: 'document',
    href: p('services/on-page-seo'),
    title: 'On-Page SEO',
    text: 'Titles, content depth, internal links and search intent. Makes it unambiguous which query each page answers.',
  },
  {
    icon: 'pen',
    href: p('services/content'),
    title: 'Content Production',
    text: 'Service, industry and location pages that actually answer the search — researched, reviewed and ready to publish.',
  },
];

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Do I have to buy these services separately?',
    a: 'No. Most businesses run a single combined programme under local SEO; individual services make sense when there is one specific bottleneck. The free audit tells you which applies.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do you work with businesses outside Türkiye?',
    a: 'Yes. Local SEO is executed remotely — we do not need to be in your city. Our base is Antalya, Türkiye, and we work in English, Turkish and German.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Would you also work with my competitor?',
    a: 'No. We work with one business per city and industry. Moving two direct competitors up at the same time is not mathematically possible.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do you measure results?',
    a: 'Grid-based map rankings, Google Business Profile interactions (searches, direction requests, calls) and Search Console data. All three are reported together — we do not send ranking screenshots on their own.',
  },
];
---

<ContentLayout
  locale={locale}
  route="services"
  title="Local SEO Services | Wiserrise"
  description="Google Business Profile management, local SEO, map rank tracking, technical SEO, on-page SEO and content production for local businesses."
  eyebrow="Services"
  h1="Local SEO Services"
  lede="Three things decide where you rank on Maps: the strength of your profile, the strength of your site, and the trust signals coming from everywhere else. Each service below addresses one of them."
  serviceName="Local SEO and Google Maps Optimization"
  breadcrumb={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }]}
  faq={faq}
  wideFirst
  ctaLabel="Get My Free Audit →"
  ctaHref="/#contact"
>
  <p>
    Local competition happens in a much narrower space than most businesses assume. When
    someone opens their phone and types <strong>"lawyer near me"</strong> or
    <strong>"car inspection Bursa"</strong>, Google scans a radius of a few kilometres around
    that person and shows three businesses. The fourth result is technically "on page one"
    and practically invisible.
  </p>

  <p>
    Getting into those three is not one intervention. The signals Google weighs in local
    ranking fall into roughly three groups, and our services map onto those groups.
  </p>

  <h2 id="three-layers">The three layers that decide ranking</h2>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Layer</th>
          <th>What Google looks at</th>
          <th>Related service</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Profile</strong></td>
          <td>Category accuracy, service list, photo and post freshness, review volume and wording</td>
          <td><a href={p('services/gbp')}>Google Business Profile Management</a></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Site</strong></td>
          <td>Crawlability, page speed, structured data, page-to-query match, content depth</td>
          <td>
            <a href={p('services/technical-seo')}>Technical SEO</a>,
            <a href={p('services/on-page-seo')}>On-Page SEO</a>,
            <a href={p('services/content')}>Content Production</a>
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>External signals</strong></td>
          <td>Name and phone consistency across directories, mentions, links</td>
          <td><a href={p('services/local-seo')}>Local SEO</a></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Investing in one layer while another is broken is the most common way we see money
    wasted. A business with a fast site and good content can stay invisible for months
    because it picked the wrong primary category. The reverse is just as common: a flawless
    profile attached to a site Google cannot index will not reach the top three in a
    competitive city.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>Don't guess where to start.</strong> The free audit measures all three layers
      and shows you which one is holding you back. No obligation to work with us afterwards.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Fragment slot="wide">
    <h2 id="services" class="section-lead">Services</h2>
    <p class="section-lead-sub">
      Each one addresses one of the three layers that decide local ranking.
    </p>
    <CardGrid items={services} cta="Read more" />
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="cta">
    <h2>Not sure which one you <span class="highlight">need</span>?</h2>
    <p>The free audit reviews your profile, your site and your competitors, and tells you.</p>
  </Fragment>
</ContentLayout>
